{"question": "Why does the arm feel heavy to move in Freedrive near some positions?", "answer": "[1] The arm feels heavy to move in Freedrive near some positions because the robot approaches its joint limits or singularity positions. As it gets closer to these positions, the resistance increases, making it feel heavier. This is a safety feature to prevent the robot from colliding with objects or exceeding its operational limits.\n\n[2] The heavy feeling can also be due to the LED indicating that the robot is approaching a singularity position, which increases the resistance.\n\n[3] Backdrive, another mode that can make the arm feel heavy, is used to prevent vibrations during a full restart when the robot is close to a collision.\n\n[4] Ensure that the payload settings and robot mounting settings are correctly configured before using Freedrive to avoid unexpected movements.\n\n[5] Verify that the installation settings, such as the robot mounting angle, payload mass, and payload center of gravity offset, are correct to prevent the risk of the robot arm falling during Freedrive.\n\nI could not find this in the documentation I have. [1][2][3][4]", "model": "qwen2.5:7b", "pipeline": "agent", "retrieval": "hybrid+rerank", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "search_docs", "args": {"source": "all", "query": "heavy arm in Freedrive"}, "n_sources": 5}], "flags": [], "sources": [{"n": 1, "doc": "UR3e_User_Manual_en_SW5.24.pdf", "page": 60, "title": "7.7. Freedrive", "id": "ur_manual_en:p60:0"}, {"n": 2, "doc": "UR3e_User_Manual_en_SW5.24.pdf", "page": 62, "title": "7.7.1. Freedrive Panel", "id": "ur_manual_en:p62:0"}, {"n": 3, "doc": "UR3e_User_Manual_en_SW5.24.pdf", "page": 200, "title": "15.3.2. Backdrive", "id": "ur_manual_en:p200:1"}, {"n": 4, "doc": "UR3e_User_Manual_de_SW5.24.pdf", "page": 69, "title": "7.7.1. Freedrive-Panel", "id": "ur_manual_de:p69:0"}, {"n": 5, "doc": "UR3e_User_Manual_en_SW5.24.pdf", "page": 169, "title": "11. The First Program", "id": "ur_manual_en:p169:0"}],